.TH CCOL_CIRCQ_TIMED_SEND_ZC 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_circq_timed_send_zc \- send a message with a timeout
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_circq_timed_send_zc(ccol_circular_queue *cq,
                                   c_message_t *msg,
                                   uint64_t timeout_us);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function blocks and waits for free space for at most the given
timeout. If free space arrives, the function sends the message and
transfers the ownership; if the timeout ends, or if sends go off, the
function returns the matching error code.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cq          Circular queue to send to
msg         Message to send. On success the ownership of the data moves to
            the queue.
timeout_us  Longest time to wait, in microseconds, from the call. 0
            does not wait, which makes the call ccol_circq_try_send_zc.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success              - The function sent the message
ccol_invalid_args         - cq or msg is NULL, or the check of msg
                            fails
ccol_not_permitted        - Sends on the queue are turned off
ccol_container_full       - The timeout is 0 and the queue is full
ccol_timed_out            - The timeout ended
ccol_unexpected_failure   - The wait failed with an error other than a
                            timeout; errno holds that error
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function blocks for the duration of the timeout at most.
After the function wakes up, it checks again whether sends are on.
On failure, the caller keeps the ownership of msg->data.
A timeout of 0 does not wait: the call then does exactly what
ccol_circq_try_send_zc does and returns its result, so it gives
ccol_container_full, not ccol_timed_out, when it cannot proceed at
once.
A timeout too large to form a deadline, such as UINT64_MAX, saturates
to the latest deadline that the clock can hold and in practice waits
with no end; it never wraps into the past.
The library measures the timeout against CLOCK_MONOTONIC, so a change
to the wall clock of the system (by an administrator, by NTP, or by the
resume of a virtual machine) does not make the timeout longer or
shorter: the call waits for the duration that the caller gave.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_circq_send_zc (3),
.BR ccol_circq_try_send_zc (3)
